1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,845
Chapter One starts with three scores: one, two, and three.

2
00:00:03,845 --> 00:00:10,806
Before softmax turns them into probabilities, the course code subtracts the largest score from all three.

3
00:00:10,806 --> 00:00:12,795
Why change every number first?

4
00:00:12,795 --> 00:00:19,888
In the next few minutes, I will show the calculation, the reason it works, and one thing it does not prove.

5
00:00:19,888 --> 00:00:23,998
This narration is synthetic and generated locally with Kokoro.

6
00:00:23,998 --> 00:00:25,911
Softmax does three things.

7
00:00:25,911 --> 00:00:28,191
First, exponentiate each score.

8
00:00:28,191 --> 00:00:30,987
Second, add those exponential weights.

9
00:00:30,987 --> 00:00:34,371
Third, divide each weight by the shared total.

10
00:00:34,371 --> 00:00:37,314
The results are positive and sum to one.

11
00:00:37,314 --> 00:00:39,742
A score is not yet a probability.

12
00:00:39,742 --> 00:00:44,156
Softmax is the transformation that creates the distribution.

13
00:00:44,156 --> 00:00:48,459
I ran the Chapter One reference program exactly as provided.

14
00:00:48,459 --> 00:01:00,293
For scores one, two, and three, it prints these probabilities: about zero point zero nine zero zero, zero point two four four seven, and zero point six six five two.

15
00:01:00,293 --> 00:01:05,098
These are recorded outputs, not numbers invented for the animation.

16
00:01:05,098 --> 00:01:09,186
The largest score still receives the largest probability.

17
00:01:09,186 --> 00:01:10,651
The maximum is three.

18
00:01:10,651 --> 00:01:12,884
Subtract three from every score.

19
00:01:12,884 --> 00:01:14,628
One becomes negative two.

20
00:01:14,628 --> 00:01:16,372
Two becomes negative one.

21
00:01:16,372 --> 00:01:17,698
Three becomes zero.

22
00:01:17,698 --> 00:01:24,744
Notice what stays fixed: the gap from one score to the next is still one, and the order is unchanged.

23
00:01:24,744 --> 00:01:30,256
We moved the whole group together; we did not change their relative preference.

24
00:01:30,256 --> 00:01:34,090
Now exponentiate negative two, negative one, and zero.

25
00:01:34,090 --> 00:01:41,473
The weights are about zero point one three five three, zero point three six seven nine, and exactly one.

26
00:01:41,473 --> 00:01:45,094
Their total is about one point five zero three two.

27
00:01:45,094 --> 00:01:51,554
Dividing by that total produces the same three probabilities printed by the course program.

28
00:01:51,554 --> 00:01:53,400
The intermediates changed.

29
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:55,814
The intended distribution did not.

30
00:01:55,814 --> 00:02:00,772
The lesson also tests two equal scores: one thousand and one thousand.

31
00:02:00,772 --> 00:02:04,597
Subtracting the maximum turns them into zero and zero.

32
00:02:04,597 --> 00:02:06,935
Exponentiation gives one and one.

33
00:02:06,935 --> 00:02:09,910
Normalization gives one half and one half.

34
00:02:09,910 --> 00:02:19,260
The shared offset of one thousand carried no information about which option was preferred, so removing it changes nothing important.

35
00:02:19,260 --> 00:02:21,249
Here is the general reason.

36
00:02:21,249 --> 00:02:28,100
Exponential of z sub i minus m equals exponential of z sub i times exponential of negative m.

37
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:31,120
That same factor appears in every weight.

38
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:36,644
It can be factored out of the denominator and cancelled with the numerator.

39
00:02:36,644 --> 00:02:42,243
This is why subtracting one shared constant preserves the normalized ratios.

40
00:02:42,243 --> 00:02:46,810
It is an algebraic identity, not a coincidence in one example.

41
00:02:46,810 --> 00:02:55,838
This explanation establishes a narrower claim: max subtraction avoids unnecessarily large positive exponentials in this calculation.

42
00:02:55,838 --> 00:02:59,600
It does not prove that the original scores are correct.

43
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:02,609
It does not make the most likely token true.

44
00:03:02,609 --> 00:03:10,133
And, as Chapter One warns, it does not prove the teaching function is safe for every imaginable extreme input.

45
00:03:10,133 --> 00:03:12,800
Stable arithmetic is not an answer key.
